meta {
  name: inbound (opt-out)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/internal/rcs/inbound
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "from": "+15551234567",
    "text": "STOP"
  }
}

docs {
  Handles a reply that came back from a recipient, and opts them out if it is a
  STOP.

  **You do not normally call this.** When a phone is paired, incoming messages
  are delivered to this logic in-process — there is no network hop. This request
  exists so the opt-out behaviour can be exercised without a real handset.

  ## What counts as an opt-out

  The **whole message** must be one of the standard keywords, after stripping
  case and punctuation: stop, stopall, stop all, unsubscribe, cancel, end, quit,
  optout, opt out, revoke.

  So `STOP`, `stop!` and ` Unsubscribe. ` all opt out. `please stop by the
  store` and `can i cancel my order` do **not**. Substring matching would
  unsubscribe people who never asked, which is worse than missing one.

  ## What happens on a match

  The number is handed to the configured opt-out store (`rcs.Blocker`), the
  do-not-text list future sends should respect.

  ```json
  {
    "success": true,
    "message": "opted out",
    "data": { "from": "+15551234567", "optOut": true, "blocked": true }
  }
  ```

  **`blocked` tells you whether the opt-out store actually recorded it.** When
  no store is configured, which is the default here, the
  call is skipped and you get `"opt-out seen but not recorded"` with
  `blocked: false`. Do not read that as suppression having happened — the
  recipient will still be texted.

  Errors:
  - 400 — `from` is not E.164
  - 502 — the opt-out store rejected it or could not be reached
}
